prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a first-person shooter (FPS) game. The player uses a ray gun to eliminate enemy blocks in a multi-room arena.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules within <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { PointerLockControls } from 'three/addons/controls/PointerLockControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Arena Construction]
(1) Use BoxGeometry walls to build 3 interconnected rooms (each approximately 20×20, connected through 4-unit-wide doorways), with large planes for the floor and ceiling;
(2) Use dark-colored MeshStandardMaterial for the walls, gray for the floor, and a light color for the ceiling;
(3) Each room must have a differently colored PointLight to distinguish its atmosphere;

[Player Controls]
(4) Use PointerLockControls to implement mouse-look controls (click the canvas to lock the pointer);
(5) Use WASD for movement, with a movement speed of 8 units/s;
(6) Movement requires wall collision detection: cast a short-range Raycaster (distance approximately 1.0) along the player's movement direction, and prevent movement in that direction when it hits a wall;
(7) Fix the camera height at y=1.6 (simulating eye level);

[Enemy System]
(8) Place 10 enemy blocks (BoxGeometry 1×2×1) throughout the 3 rooms, using red materials;
(9) Each enemy has HP=100 and displays an HP bar above its head (using PlaneGeometry + dynamic color or a Canvas texture), always facing the camera to create a billboard effect;
(10) Enemy AI: slowly rotate to face the player; when the distance to the player is < 30 units, move toward the player at a speed of 1.5 units/s;
(11) When an enemy dies (HP<=0): change its material to gray, make it fall to one side (rotate 90° around the Z axis), and stop its AI behavior;

[Weapon System]
(12) Draw a crosshair at the center of the screen (implemented with DOM or CSS, as a white +);
(13) Shoot on left-click: cast a Raycaster from the center of the camera along the viewing direction and detect living enemies within a range of 50 units;
(14) On a hit, reduce the enemy's HP by 25;
(15) Ammunition system: each magazine holds 30 rounds and the count must be displayed on the HUD; ammo-1 when firing;
(16) When ammo=0, shooting has no effect. Press the R key to reload; reloading takes 2 seconds (during this period isReloading=true and shooting is disabled);

[HUD Display]
(17) Display the following in the upper-left corner:
  - Player health (id="playerHP")
  - Ammunition count (id="ammo", format "current/30")
  - Kill count (id="kills")
  - Number of living enemies (id="enemiesAlive")

[State Exposure]
(18) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame, containing:
  - playerHP: number (player health, initially 100)
  - ammo: number (current ammunition count)
  - maxAmmo: number (magazine capacity=30)
  - kills: number (kill count)
  - enemiesAlive: number (number of living enemies)
  - playerPos: { x, y, z } (player position)
  - enemies: [{ x, y, z, hp, alive }] (state array for the 10 enemies)
  - isReloading: boolean (whether reloading is currently in progress)

Output a single HTML file that can run when opened directly in a browser.
